            <ArticleTitle>Delayed-onset post-craniotomy headache responsive to botulinum toxin A: a case series.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1375-1378</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00701-017-3215-z</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report here the case of four patients presenting with delayed-onset temporal pain after pterional craniotomy. They reported similar symptoms: attacks of pain over the temporal region, ipsilateral to the operative site, irradiating around the eye and lasting from 10 min to 1 h. All patients had hypertrophy of at least one part of the temporalis muscle. All responded dramatically to botulinum toxin A injection (25 to 50 Botox® units) into the temporalis muscle. We suggest that the headaches were caused by aberrant nerve regeneration following surgical injury to the frontal branch of the facial nerve.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
